27</volume><fpage>19</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17234996</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Chest CT: <bold>A</bold>) Coronal section shows low attenuation of the pericardial fat (arrow). An area of pathologic fat necrosis and inflammation is seen lateral to the pericardial fat, corresponding to the shadow seen on admission CXR. An axial section (<bold>B</bold>), shows the necrotic area (arrow). Six months later (<bold>C</bold>), the lesion measured 1.4 cm</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="AMS-7-2-337_F1"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table I</label><caption><p>Previous cases of epipericardial fat necrosis</p></caption><table frame="border" rules="rows" width="100%"><thead><tr><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Case</th><th valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">First author</th><th valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Year<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="T1F1">*</xref></th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age [years]</th><th valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sex</th><th valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BMI [kg/m<sup>2</sup>]</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Complaint</th><th valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Size [cm]</th><th valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Jackson</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1957</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">52</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">M</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">29.9</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Right-sided chest pain</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5 &#x000d7; 2.5 &#x000d7; 2</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Exploratory thoracotomy</td></tr><tr><td 